ligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $P(1,2)$ to point $Q(4,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbd34e81-f803-5fbc-b7c5-b362f720fe60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259705 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349219 #edb/ref 1080 "2+6\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5480a9aa-ad9e-5901-99ef-a4b557010682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349782 #edb/ref 1080 "2+10\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349782 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349782 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9515cda8-658c-596e-82bc-d1176ae74422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350330 #edb/ref 1080 "1+12\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350330 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350330 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d708ba15-3640-5e0f-a2cc-8efebb555009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350495 #e