\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $B(-\\sqrt{3},0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8b59c59-a390-5b22-8421-70bf95742c06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387540 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$